; The 64-bit decimal (VarkaDecimalCompiler, VARKA-348). A DECIMAL(p, s) value on the long
; lane is an unscaled long u, the value u / 10^s, with |u| <= 10^p - 1 on every valid lane
; (the leaf's slot check for a column, decimal_slot.smt2). The compiler builds:
;
;   rescale by k digits:  IntArith(MUL, WRAP, u, 10^k), when p + k <= 18     jlong.mul
;   a comparison:         Compare(op, u1 * 10^k, u2), s2 = s1 + k            <, <=, =
;   negation:             IntNeg(WRAP, u)                                   jlong.neg
;   abs, the blend:       IfElse(u < 0, IntNeg(WRAP, u), u)
;   abs, lanewise ABS:    Math.abs's definition, u < 0 ? -u : u, wrapping
;
; Every claim up to the arithmetic below is linear, since every multiplier is a constant;
; the product's bound there multiplies two unknowns. The rescale is proven at every (p, k)
; with p + k <= 18; a narrower operand's range is inside the widest one's, so the
; comparison lemma is stated at the widest left operand each k allows.
;
; Rendered by VarkaProofFiles. VarkaProofFilesSuite fails when this file differs from its
; rendering, and VARKA_PROOFS_REGEN=true build/sbt 'catalyst/testOnly *VarkaProofFilesSuite'
; rewrites it. Run by dev/varka_prove.sh, which inserts java.smt2 after the set-logic line.

(set-logic QF_NIA)

(define-fun dec.in ((u Int) (b Int)) Bool (and (<= (- b) u) (<= u b)))
(define-fun dec.absBlend ((u Int)) Int (ite (< u 0) (jlong.neg u) u))
(define-fun dec.absLanewise ((u Int)) Int (jlong.wrap (ite (< u 0) (- u) u)))

(echo "p = 1, k = 1: |u| <= 9 gives u * 10 with no wrap, within 2 digits: expect unsat")
(push 1)
(declare-const u Int)
(assert (dec.in u 9))
(assert (not (and (= (jlong.mul u 10) (* u 10)) (dec.in (* u 10) 99))))
(check-sat)
(pop 1)

